^bo^un1. Scope^un^bo

This document defines the external specifications of GRiD Network Manager (called Network Manager in the remainder of this document). Network Manager provides a central framework for configuring a GRiD Network and controls allocation of network resources to applications wishing to utilize the GRiD Server's communications capabilities.

^boTHOSE FEATURES OF NETWORK MANAGER THAT ARE NOT PART OF THE FIRST RELEASE ARE FLAGGED WITH AN ASTERISK (*).^bo

^bo1.1 Terminology^bo

Refer to GRiDMail Overview Specification (Doc. No. 800077).

^bo1.2 References^bo

Refer to GRiDMail Overview Specification (Doc. No. 800077).

^bo1.3 Open Issues^bo

	(1)	What should Network Manager do if the option to exit after loading is set to 'Yes'? Should a stub always be present in memory for configuration purposes?
^ep
^he "Network Manager ERS - Preliminary""""Functional Description"
^bo^un2. Functional Description^un^bo

^bo2.1 Network Manager Overview^bo

Network Manager provides a central framework for configuring a GRiD Network and controls allocation of network resources to applications wishing to utilize the GRiD Server's communications capabilities. This is accomplished by maintaining a central database of information about network configuration and keeping track of the current state of the network.

A ^itGRiD Network^it comprises ^itRemote Systems^it, ^itwork stations^it, and ^itapplications^it. The only remote systems known to Network Manager are GRiD Servers. A GRiD Server allows users to sign on from work stations and access the server's resources. Direct connections between work stations are not supported. Applications resident in a GRiD Server may sign on to other GRiD Servers in exactly the same way as users, using a ^itnetwork link^it (or ^itconnection^it) configured for this purpose. Of course, an application can sign on to its own GRiD Server - this does not require a network link.

A GRiD Server or any other remote system is identified by its ^itremote system name^it. A network link or connection is identified by its ^itlink name^it (or ^it connection name^it). Network Manager maintains mappings between remote system names and sets of link names, and between link names and link properties. The set of link names associated with a remote system name, called the ^itaccess link set^it, identifies the network links through which the remote system can be accessed. The link properties associated with a network link specify the link protocol, protocol parameters, and ^itsystem port^it. Three link protocols are supported - GRiDLink Primary, GRiDLink Secondary (*), and Phonelink. There are three flavors of Phonelink. GRiDLink Primary and Phonelink ^itAnswer^it permit users (or applications on other GRiD Servers) to sign on. GRiDLink Secondary (*) and ^itPhonelink Originate^it allow server resident applications to sign on to other GRiD Servers. Phonelink ^itOriginate and Answer^it allows a phone line to be used for dialing out as well as accepting calls. Note that the Phonelink (Originate, Answer, and Originate and Answer) protocols do not allow more than one user or application to use the network link at the same time.

Network Manager also maintains mappings between system ports (or physical ports, or simply, ports) and port properties. Port properties reflect the actual hardware configured at the port, and specify the protocol the port is to handle. Sometimes the hardware is implicitly specified by the protocol (for example, GRiDLink).

^bo2.2  Network Manager Operation^bo

Like Admin Manager and VFS Manager, Network Manager is an essential application that gets started when a GRiD Server is powered on or reset. Network Manager does not have an installation phase. Upon starting, Network Manager registers the names 'Network Manager~Manager~' and 'Network Manager~Standard~' in the name device. If a configuration file is not found, Network Manager creates a default configuration file, and puts the default configuration into effect. The default configuration loads the first communications server card with Phonelink Answer at ports 0 and 1, and GRiDLink Primary at ports 2 and 3. At this point the network administrator should connect to Network Manager using RemoteAdministrator and create the desired network configuration. ^itNote: In the first release, the GRiD Server may have to be reset in order for the configuration to take effect, under certain circumstances. If the GRiD Server has to be reset, Network Manager will display a message to that effect.^it

There are two phases to Network Manager configuration - configuration of the communications server cards and system ports, and configuration of remote systems and their access link sets.

A communications server card supports four ports. A port is identified by the pair (x, y), where x is the card number (2-7) and y is the line number (0-3). Within a card, a port is unambiguously identified by just the line number. For each port, the administrator specifies a protocol type, certain protocol parameters, and hardware related information (such as modem type). There are some restrictions on protocol combinations supported by a communications server card - these restrictions are detailed in a later section.

In order to facilitate signing on to other GRiD Servers, Network Manager allows specification of GRiD Servers that are part of the network (as remote systems). For each remote system, the administrator specifies an access link set through which the remote system may be accessed. Thus, an application wishing to sign on to a GRiD Server need only specify the remote system name corresponding to that GRiD Server (and optionally, more parameters) in the sign-on command.

A network link or connection is a logical entity that is dynamically bound to a port at sign-on time. For each network link, the administrator specifies a protocol type, telephone numbers if the protocol involves dialing, certain protocol related parameters, and a set of ports the network link may get mapped on to at sign-on time. The protocol types specified for the ports must match the protocol type specified for the network link.

A global option specifies whether Network Manager should exit after loading the communications server cards with the appropriate protocol machines. By default, Network Manager does not exit. The purpose of this option is to let Network Manager exit in minimal GRiD Server configurations that don't require GRiD Server to GRiD Server networking. Other global parameters specify thresholds for low speed, medium speed, and high speed lines.

Network Manager supports an application interface, different from the configuration interface, that allows applications to allocate network links, free allocated links, and query Network Manager for network related information. Through this interface, applications can get lists of all remote systems, network links in a given remote system's access link set, network link parameters for a given network link, and port parameters for a given port. Sophisticated applications may look into network link parameters, and select a network link with the desired parameters.

^bo2.3 Configuration Interface^bo

After connecting to Network Manager via RemoteAdministrator, the first form seen by the administrator is the password form:

		Enter Password:

This form is skipped if the administrator also happens to be the system administrator.

Next, the main menu of Network Manager is presented:

					Configure a communications server card
					Add a connection to a remote system
					Modify a connection to a remote system
		(CODE-E)	Erase a connection to a remote system
		(CODE-O)	Edit options
					Change password
		(CODE-T)	Print configuration, save configuration
		(CODE-Q)	Quit, save configuration changes
		(CODE-ESC)	Cancel, don't save changes

Note that the word 'connection' in the above form denotes a network link, and 'remote system' denotes any remote processor the network link connects to, including GRiD Servers.

^bo2.3.1 Configure a communications server card^bo

Selecting this command results in the following menu to be displayed:

	Select card to configure:

	Card2 (128K)
	Card3 (512k)
	etc.

The exact format of the above menu is TBD. After a card is selected for configuration, the form for card parameters is presented:

	Enter card parameters:

	Port0 protocol type:
	Port1 protocol type:
	Port2 protocol type:
	Port3 protocol type:

Choices for protocol types are the three protocol types supported and 'other'. For each port whose protocol type is Phonelink, an additional form is presented:

	Enter port^itx^it parameters:

	Phonelink Type:     [Originate, Answer, Originate and Answer]
	Line Type:			[Switched, Leased]
	Speed:				[choices are baud rates]
	Modem type:			[Smartmodem-1200,  Other]
	Phone number prefix:[a string]
	Dial mode:			[pulse, touch-tone]
	Dial type:			[manual, automatic]

If the line type is leased, there is no dialing involved in establishing connection: therefore phone number prefix, dial mode, and dial type are ignored. The phone number is prefixed with the phone number prefix - this is useful for getting an outside line in local switchboard operation.

If the protocol type specified is 'other', the port can be used by an application in a manner unknown to Network Manager. If all ports in a card have their protocol types set to 'other', the whole card is reserved for applications not involving Network Manager. ^itNote: In the first release, protocol types for all ports on a card must be set to 'other' in order for an application to use one or more ports on that card for non-networking purposes.^it

The following table illustrates the restrictions on protocol combinations supported by a card. Type A stands for Phonelink types, and Type B stands for GRiDLink (Primary or Secondary).

