^bo2.5 Application Interface^bo

This section describes a procedural interface (available as a link module) for Network Manager's application interface.

Network Manager supports an application interface that allows server applications to allocate network links, free allocated links, and query Network Manager for network related information. Through this interface, applications can get lists of all remote systems, network links in a given remote system's access link set, or network link parameters for a given network link. 

^bo2.5.1 Allocate Link^bo

AllocateLink: PROCEDURE (pProperties, propLength, pRetProps,
								length, pRetLength, pError);
DCL	pProperties	POINTER,
	propLength	WORD,
	pRetProps	POINTER,
	length		WORD,
	pRetLength	POINTER,
	pError		POINTER;

The parameters are:

pProperties:	(INPUT) Pointer to a list of properties.
				Property formats and the properties required
				for this command are described below.

propLength:		(INPUT) Total length of above list.

pRetProps:		(OUTPUT) Pointer to buffer containing returned
				properties (provided by user).

length:			(INPUT) Length of buffer pointed to by
				pRetProps.

pRetLength:		(OUTPUT) Pointer to word containing total
				length of returned properties.

pError:			(OUTPUT) Pointer to a word containing returned
				error code. Possible Error values are:
					Invalid parameter	- TBD
					Invalid command		- TBD
					Link is in use		- TBD


Each property has the following format:

	+------+------+------+------+------------
	| 0FDH |   Length    | Type | Property
	+------+------+------+------+------------

Length is a word that specifies the length (in bytes) of the remainder of the record. Type is the property type, and Property is the property itself. The properties that go with this command are: Remote System name, network link (or connection) name, link speed, link cost, link protocol type, card number, port number, telephone number, and station number. Minimally, the command should have the GRiD Server name or the network link name (or both). Additionally, the command could have link speed, link cost and link protocol type. Alternately, the command could have the card number, port number, and station number - these server as manual overrides over automatic selection of these parameters.

The returned properties are GRiD Server name, network link name, line speed, line cost, card number, port number, and station number.

